# Contract mode — spec-driven development

One file · one loop · **zero sub-agents for the core**. Schema authority is [`../references/spec/contract-format.md`](../references/spec/contract-format.md) — read it before writing any contract. This rule is the pattern summary; **the schema reference wins on disputes**.

**Spine:** durable `SPEC.md` (survives context reset) · **concise encoding** (~75% fewer tokens than prose) · **backprop reflex** (fail → §B + often §V) · **right-size** (ceremony scales to blast radius, not ego).

**Two contract modes in this pack — pick one per effort:**

| Mode | Artifact | Skills | Use when |
| ---- | -------- | ------ | -------- |
| **Contract** | `SPEC.md` — §G…§B invariants | [`contract`](../skills/contract/SKILL.md) · [`build`](../skills/build/SKILL.md) · [`contract-check`](../skills/contract-check/SKILL.md) · [`contract-backprop`](../skills/contract-backprop/SKILL.md) | Invariants matter; long-running effort; drift audits wanted |
| **PRD** | Product doc → tickets | [`spec`](../skills/spec/SKILL.md) → [`to-tickets`](../skills/to-tickets/SKILL.md) → `build` | Feature work sliced into tracker tickets |

⊥ mix them in one effort. ⊥ invent a third format. Unsure → [`ask`](../skills/ask/SKILL.md).

**Not core (still available):** `/ship` orchestration, [delegation](./delegation.mdc) subagents, [design](./design.mdc) specialists. Those must **not** invent a second contract format — when they touch a contract they use §G…§B at the active `spec_path`.

## Why contract mode beats plan-then-execute

| Plan-then-execute | Contract mode |
| ----------------- | ------------- |
| Fixes code, forgets the lesson | **Backprop** — §B row + optional new §V |
| Contract and code drift silently | **Check** — read-only §V / §I / §T report |
| Agent swarm + dashboards | **One thread** — reading `SPEC.md` is the dashboard |
| Retry on failure without updating intent | Fail → classify → update contract → resume build |
| Prose contract burns tokens every turn | **Concise** symbols + pipe tables |

## Build contract — addressable sections

Fixed order. Address as `§V.2`, `§T.3`, etc. Full schema + symbols → [`../references/spec/contract-format.md`](../references/spec/contract-format.md).

| Section | Holds | Notes |
| ------- | ----- | ----- |
| **§G** Goal | One-line outcome | Concise fragment |
| **§C** Constraints | Non-negotiables, locks | Bullets |
| **§I** Interfaces | External surface (cmd / api / file / env) | What the world sees |
| **§R** Research | Sourced external facts | Optional; only if research ran |
| **§V** Invariants | Numbered testable rules | Each ! must hold; build names proving test |
| **§T** Tasks | Pipe table: id \| status \| task \| cites | `.` → `~` → `x`; cites §V / §I |
| **§B** Bugs | Backprop log | Cause + fix / invariant that catches recurrence |

## Sectioned ownership

No verb rewrites a section it does not own (kills “tool deleted my contract”):

| Verb | Writes |
| ---- | ------ |
| **grill** | §G + §C (propose; write on OK) |
| **contract** | Sole general editor — NEW all; AMEND named; bug → §B + §V |
| **research** | Appends §R only |
| **code-review** | Hardens §V (+ report; no silent full rewrite) |
| **build** | §T status cell only |
| **codebase-design** (deepening) | Proposes §I + §V + §T (write on OK) |
| **contract-check** | Read-only |

Cross-cutting edits → **contract**. Compacting oldest §B when the file exceeds ~500 lines → **contract** (show diff first).

## Right-size

| Change | Ceremony |
| ------ | -------- |
| One-line / typo / obvious local fix | Just **build** (or fix + §B if it was a bug) |
| New feature touching shared surface | **grill** → **contract** → **code-review** before build |
| Unknown library / API version | **research** → §R before build assumes facts |
| Uncertain or high blast-radius | Full grill → contract → research → review → build |
| Green build, spare budget, one shallow module | **codebase-design** deepening (optional) |

⊥ full chain for a typo. Skip any verb that costs more attention than the change is worth.

## Skill chain

```
idea → [grill] → contract (NEW) → human OK? → [research] → [review] → build → all §T x
                        ↑                                                ↓ fail
        amend / contract-backprop ←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←←
                        ↑
        contract-check (read-only drift audit, anytime)
```

| Skill | Mutates contract? | When |
| ----- | ----------------- | ---- |
| **contract** | Yes — sole general mutator | NEW, AMEND, DISTILL, BACKPROP / bug |
| **build** | §T status only | Plan→execute; names which test proves each touched §V |
| **contract-check** | Never | Drift report on §V / §I / §T |
| **contract-backprop** | Via `contract` | Test fail, bug report, check VIOLATE with known cause |

**The loop runs single-thread — no sub-agents.** Main context owns plan and edits. Sub-agents elsewhere (delegation, `/ship` phase agents, data-systems) do not replace this loop.

## Contract modes

| Mode | Trigger | Action |
| ---- | ------- | ------ |
| **NEW** | Greenfield idea or ticket | Write full contract per the schema reference; show user; wait for OK |
| **DISTILL** | Existing codebase, no contract | Infer §G/§C/§I/§V from code/tests |
| **AMEND** | Targeted edit | Change only the named section; show diff |
| **BACKPROP** | `bug:` prefix or failed verification | §B row → prefer new §V → optional §T update |

**Rules:**

- IDs monotonic — never reuse §V / §T / §B / §R ids.
- Every bug gets a §B row; a new §V is optional but preferred.
- Build never silently fixes contract gaps — backprop when the failure is (b) contract wrong or (c) unspecified edge.
- No auto-build after a contract write. The human confirms, then invokes build.
- Commits/PRs: plain Conventional Commits describing behavior — **⊥** `§T.n` / `§V.n` / `Tn` / `Vn` in git history (those stay in the contract and agent chat).

## Build loop

1. Read the contract at the active path (`SPEC.md`, or `/ship` `spec_path`). Missing → defer to **contract**.
2. Load the schema reference once if not in context.
3. **Plan** — cite every applicable §V and §I; list files + tests; **name the verification command that proves each touched §V**.
4. **Execute** per §T: `.` → `~` → edit → verify → `x` or backprop.
5. A task is `x` only when verification exits 0 and tests cover the cited invariants.
6. **Commit** after each completed task (plain Conventional Commits).

## Check (drift audit)

Read-only. Classifies each §V: **HOLD** / **VIOLATE** / **UNVERIFIABLE**. Each §I: **MATCH** / **DRIFT** / **MISSING** / **EXTRA**. Each done §T: evidence or **STALE**.

Ends with remedy hints (invoke **contract** or **build**) — never auto-fixes.

## Backprop (the differentiator)

Six steps on every meaningful failure:

1. **Trace** — `file:line` of wrong behavior; one-sentence root cause.
2. **Analyze** — would a new §V catch this class? Is §I wrong? Is §T wrong?
3. **Propose** — §B row + draft §V (testable, positive, concise).
4. **Generate test** — an invariant without a test is fiction; name the test after the **behavior** (⊥ `§V` ids in product test names).
5. **Verify** — fix code; test green; full suite green.
6. **Log** — one commit: contract + test + fix.

Skip a new §V only for one-off typos, one-time migrations, or external-dependency issues (still log §B).

## Good invariant shape

| Bad | Good (concise) |
| --- | -------------- |
| "Code should be correct" | `V1: ∀ DB query → parameterized bindings — ⊥ string-concat user input` |
| "Handle errors" | `V2: ∀ HTTP handler → domain err → typed response — ⊥ empty catch` |

Invariants are grep-able or assert-able, scoped to behavior, and cite §I where they apply.

